      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2017 SEICAP. Published by Elsevier España, S.L.U. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Several countries or regions within countries have an effective national asthma strategy resulting in a reduction of the large burden of asthma to individuals and society. There has been no systematic appraisal of the extent of national asthma strategies in the world."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The Global Asthma Network (GAN) undertook an email survey of 276 Principal Investigators of GAN centres in 120 countries, in 2013-2014. One of the questions was: \"Has a national asthma strategy been developed in your country for the next five years? For children? For adults?\"."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Investigators in 112 (93.3%) countries answered this question. Of these, 26 (23.2%) reported having a national asthma strategy for children and 24 (21.4%) for adults; 22 (19.6%) countries had a strategy for both children and adults; 28 (25%) had a strategy for at least one age group. In countries with a high prevalence of current wheeze, strategies were significantly more common than in low prevalence countries (11/13 (85%) and 7/31 (22.6%) respectively, p<0.001)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "INTERPRETATION",
            "content": "In 25% countries a national asthma strategy was reported. A large reduction in the global burden of asthma could be potentially achieved if more countries had an effective asthma strategy."
          }
        ]
      },
